# Matter pages: trademarks and related intellectual property (copyright, design rights). No em dashes, colons or semicolons.
# Trade mark work is not a reserved legal activity, but Leaflegal only introduces SRA regulated solicitors and IPReg regulated
# chartered trade mark attorneys. General information only, never advice. UK wide (one UK register at the UK IPO).
from matters_property import m

TRADEMARKS = [
    m('trademark-registration', 'Trademark registration', 'Registering your brand name, logo or slogan as a UK trademark.',
      ['A registered trademark gives you the exclusive right to use your brand for the goods and services it covers, across the whole of the UK. Applications are made to the UK Intellectual Property Office, which examines them, publishes them for two months so others can oppose, and then registers the mark for ten years at a time.',
       'A trademark solicitor or chartered trade mark attorney checks whether your mark is likely to be accepted, searches for earlier marks that could block it, chooses the right classes and writes the list of goods and services so it protects how you actually trade. They can also extend protection abroad, for example through the Madrid system.'],
      ['Checking whether your name or logo is distinctive enough', 'Clearance searches against earlier marks', 'Choosing classes and drafting the goods and services', 'Filing at the UK IPO and dealing with the examiner', 'International filings through the Madrid system'],
      ['The name, logo or slogan you want to protect', 'What you sell or plan to sell', 'When you started using it, if you have', 'Countries you trade in or plan to'],
      [('Can I register a trademark myself?', 'Yes. Anyone can apply online at the UK IPO. Many businesses use a professional because a badly drafted specification or the wrong classes can leave a brand poorly protected, and mistakes can’t be fixed once the application is filed.'),
       ('How long does registration take?', 'If there are no objections or oppositions, a UK application usually takes around four months from filing to registration. Objections or an opposition can add several months.'),
       ('Does registering my company name protect it as a trademark?', 'No. Registering a company at Companies House does not give you trademark rights. A trademark registration is a separate application to the UK IPO.')],
      'Registration enquiries come from founders and growing brands who are ready to file, which makes them quick, clear instructions.'),
    m('trademark-searches', 'Trademark searches', 'Clearance searches to check a brand is free to use before you launch or file.',
      ['Before you invest in a new name, logo or product range, it pays to know whether someone else already has rights in something similar. The UK IPO’s free search tool shows identical marks, but it won’t reliably find marks that are similar in sound, look or meaning, unregistered rights or company names that could cause a problem.',
       'A trademark professional runs a clearance search across the UK register and, where needed, international registers, company names and the wider market. They then give you a written view on the risks of using and registering your brand, so you can decide before you print or launch.'],
      ['UK and international register searches', 'Similar sounding and similar looking marks', 'Company names and unregistered use', 'A written opinion on risk', 'Ongoing watch services after you file'],
      ['The name or logo you want to check', 'What you will sell under it', 'Where you will trade', 'Your launch date, if you have one'],
      [('Isn’t the free UK IPO search enough?', 'It is a good first check, but it mainly finds identical or near identical marks. A professional search also looks for similar marks and judges how likely they are to cause a problem.'),
       ('When should I search?', 'Before you spend money on branding, packaging or a website, and before you file a trademark application.'),
       ('What is a trademark watch?', 'A service that alerts you when someone applies for a mark similar to yours, so you can oppose it in time.')],
      'Search enquiries are often the first step for a new brand and lead naturally into a filing instruction.'),
    m('objections-and-oppositions', 'Objections and oppositions', 'Responding to an examiner’s objection, or opposing or defending an opposition.',
      ['After you apply, the UK IPO examiner may object, for example because the mark describes the goods or is not distinctive. Once the application is published, anyone with earlier rights has two months, extendable to three, to oppose it. Owners also oppose other people’s applications that are too close to their own brand.',
       'A trademark solicitor or attorney can answer an examiner’s report, negotiate with the other side, narrow the specification or argue the case before the UK IPO tribunal. Many oppositions settle, often through a coexistence agreement, before a hearing.'],
      ['Responding to examination reports', 'Filing a notice of threatened opposition', 'Opposing an application that conflicts with your brand', 'Defending your application against an opposition', 'Negotiating coexistence agreements'],
      ['The application number and the letter or notice you received', 'Any deadline in it', 'Evidence of how long and where you have used your mark', 'Details of the other party, if you know them'],
      [('How long do I have to oppose a trademark?', 'Two months from publication in the Trade Marks Journal, which can be extended to three months by filing a notice of threatened opposition within the first two months.'),
       ('What happens if I miss the deadline?', 'You may still be able to apply to invalidate the mark after it is registered, but that is usually slower and more expensive.'),
       ('Do oppositions always go to a hearing?', 'No. Many are settled by agreement, and some are decided on the papers without a hearing.')],
      'Oppositions run to fixed UK IPO deadlines, so these clients need help quickly and are ready to instruct.'),
    m('trademark-infringement', 'Trademark infringement', 'When someone is using your brand, or you are accused of using theirs.',
      ['Trademark infringement happens when someone uses a sign that is identical or confusingly similar to a registered mark, for similar goods or services, without permission. If your mark is not registered, you may still have a claim in passing off, which protects the goodwill a business has built up.',
       'A trademark solicitor can look at the strength of your rights and the evidence of use, send a formal letter setting out your rights, ask online marketplaces to remove listings and, if needed, bring a claim in the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court or the High Court. If you have been accused, they can tell you how strong the claim is and how to respond.'],
      ['Assessing your rights and the other side’s use', 'Letters before claim and responses', 'Marketplace, social media and domain name takedowns', 'Passing off claims for unregistered brands', 'Court claims, settlements and coexistence deals'],
      ['Your registration number, if registered', 'Screenshots, photos or links showing the other use', 'When you first noticed it', 'Any letters you have sent or received'],
      [('What if my trademark isn’t registered?', 'You may still be able to stop someone using a similar name through a passing off claim, if you can show goodwill, a misrepresentation and damage. It is usually harder to prove than infringement of a registered mark.'),
       ('Can I be sued for using a name I didn’t know was taken?', 'Yes. Infringement doesn’t depend on whether you knew about the earlier mark, although it can affect what remedies a court gives.'),
       ('Where are trademark cases heard?', 'In England and Wales, smaller and simpler cases often go to the Intellectual Property Enterprise Court, where costs are capped. Larger cases go to the High Court.')],
      'Infringement clients are protecting a brand they have invested in and want a firm to act quickly.'),
    m('cease-and-desist-letters', 'Cease and desist letters', 'Sending a cease and desist letter, or responding to one you have received.',
      ['A cease and desist letter tells someone to stop doing something, such as using your brand, copying your content or selling lookalike products. In trademark, copyright and design disputes it is usually the first formal step, and many disputes end there.',
       'A solicitor makes sure the letter is accurate and proportionate. This matters because UK law penalises unjustified threats of trademark, patent and design infringement, so a badly worded letter can backfire. If you have received one, a solicitor can tell you whether the claim stands up and how to reply.'],
      ['Drafting a clear, proportionate letter', 'Avoiding unjustified threats', 'Asking for undertakings, delivery up and costs', 'Responding to a letter you have received', 'Negotiating a settlement or rebrand timetable'],
      ['The letter, if you have received one, and its deadline', 'Evidence of your rights and the other use', 'What outcome you want', 'Any earlier contact between you'],
      [('Is a cease and desist letter legally binding?', 'No. It is a formal demand, not a court order. Ignoring a well founded letter can still lead to a court claim and costs.'),
       ('Can I write my own cease and desist letter?', 'You can, but in IP disputes the rules on unjustified threats mean a poorly worded letter could expose you to a claim. Many people use a solicitor for this reason.'),
       ('What should I do if I receive one?', 'Don’t ignore it and don’t reply in anger. Note the deadline and get advice on whether the claim is valid before you respond.')],
      'Cease and desist enquiries come with a deadline or an ongoing problem, which makes them urgent, fixed fee friendly work.'),
    m('trademark-renewals', 'Renewals and ownership changes', 'Renewing a trademark, recording a new owner and keeping your portfolio in order.',
      ['UK trademarks last ten years and can be renewed indefinitely, every ten years, as long as the renewal fee is paid. You can renew up to six months before the expiry date, and there is a six month late period with an extra fee. After that, the mark can be removed from the register, although restoration is sometimes possible.',
       'Trademark professionals also record changes of owner when a business is sold or restructured, update addresses, grant licences, and check whether old registrations are vulnerable to cancellation because they haven’t been used for five years.'],
      ['Renewals and late renewals', 'Restoring a lapsed registration', 'Recording assignments after a sale', 'Licences and recordals', 'Portfolio reviews and non use risks'],
      ['Your registration numbers', 'The expiry dates, if you know them', 'Details of any sale, merger or change of owner', 'Which marks you still use'],
      [('When do I need to renew my trademark?', 'Every ten years from the filing date. You can renew up to six months before expiry, or up to six months after with a late fee.'),
       ('Can a registered trademark be cancelled?', 'Yes. Anyone can apply to revoke a mark that hasn’t been genuinely used for a continuous five year period, so it helps to keep evidence of use.'),
       ('Do I need to record a new owner?', 'Yes. If you buy a business or a brand, the change of ownership should be recorded at the UK IPO so the register shows the right owner.')],
      'Renewal and recordal clients often own several marks and become long term portfolio clients.'),
    m('copyright', 'Copyright', 'Protecting your creative work and dealing with copying, online and offline.',
      ['Copyright protects original creative work, such as writing, photographs, artwork, music, film, software and website content. In the UK it arises automatically when the work is created, so there is no register to join. It usually lasts for the creator’s life plus 70 years for most written and artistic works.',
       'A copyright solicitor can help you prove ownership, agree licences and assignments, deal with someone copying your work, send takedown notices to online platforms, or respond if you are accused of using someone else’s content without permission.'],
      ['Copyright infringement claims and defences', 'Online takedown notices', 'Licences, assignments and commissioning terms', 'Ownership disputes between creators and clients', 'Image and photo use claims'],
      ['The work and proof you created it, with dates', 'Examples of the copying', 'Any contracts or licences', 'Letters or invoices you have received'],
      [('Do I need to register copyright in the UK?', 'No. Copyright arises automatically. It helps to keep dated drafts, files and records so you can prove you created the work and when.'),
       ('I used a photo from the internet and got a bill. What now?', 'Don’t ignore it. A solicitor can check whether the claim and the amount are justified and help you respond.'),
       ('Who owns work I paid a freelancer to create?', 'Usually the freelancer, unless there is a written assignment. Employees’ work made in the course of employment usually belongs to the employer.')],
      'Copyright enquiries range from creators protecting their work to businesses facing image claims, both of which want clear fixed fees.'),
    m('design-rights', 'Design rights', 'Protecting the look of a product, and acting on copies.',
      ['Design rights protect the appearance of a product, such as its shape, pattern, contours or decoration. A registered design gives the strongest protection, for up to 25 years in five year periods, and is applied for at the UK IPO. Unregistered design rights also arise automatically but are shorter and harder to enforce.',
       'An IP solicitor or attorney can advise whether to register, prepare the drawings and application, and act if a competitor copies your product. They can also help if you are accused of copying a design yourself.'],
      ['Registered design applications', 'Unregistered UK design right and supplementary rights', 'Dealing with copies and lookalike products', 'Licensing and selling designs', 'Defending design claims'],
      ['Images or drawings of the design', 'When it was first made public', 'Examples of any copies', 'Where you sell'],
      [('Do I need to register a design?', 'Not always, because some protection is automatic. Registration gives clearer, longer protection and makes it easier to act against copies.'),
       ('Can I register a design I have already shown publicly?', 'Often yes, within 12 months of first disclosing it, but it is best to get advice before showing it.'),
       ('How long does a registered design last?', 'Up to 25 years, renewed every five years.')],
      'Design enquiries come from product businesses protecting something they have invested in, often alongside trademark work.'),
]
